// boxcxx — <cerrno>
//
// ‼ Read this before using errno for anything.
//
// **BoxOS has no errno register, and this header does not give it one.** That
// idiom is deliberately absent from every BoxOS interface: boxlib returns an
// `error_t` from the call that failed, box::error carries it in C++, and
// several headers in the tree say so in as many words ("BoxOS has no errno
// register", box/bay.h, box/brook.h, box/error.h). Nothing in that path
// touches the object below and nothing ever will.
//
// What this header is, then, is the C standard library's contract, and only
// that: [cerrno.syn] requires a thread-local modifiable int lvalue named
// `errno`, and the C functions boxcxx provides — strtod and the rest of the
// strto* family, and the math functions on the rare paths where they choose to
// — are specified to set it. A caller that writes `errno = 0; x = strtod(s,
// &e); if (errno == ERANGE)` is writing correct C, and it has to work. The
// object exists to make those specifications true, not to become a second way
// for BoxOS to report failure.
//
// The macro values are NOT invented here. They are the values of the
// corresponding std::errc enumerators in <__bits/errc>, which follow the Linux
// numbering (the standard leaves them implementation-defined and that set is
// the de-facto convention). [syserr.errcat.objects]/2 makes the two answer for
// each other: error_code(EDOM, generic_category()) == errc::argument_out_of_
// domain has to hold. Phase203 pins every pair by static_assert, which is a
// check neither reference implementation can make — theirs come from a libc
// the library does not compile against.
#ifndef BOXCXX_CERRNO
#define BOXCXX_CERRNO

// The object. thread_local because [errno] has required exactly that since
// C++11 — two strands parsing two strings must not overwrite each other's
// failure. `inline` gives the whole program one of them, in .tbss, with no
// dynamic initialization to order against anything.
inline thread_local int __boxcxx_errno = 0;

#define errno ::__boxcxx_errno

// ── The three [cerrno.syn] mandates ─────────────────────────────────────
#define EDOM   33
#define ERANGE 34
#define EILSEQ 84

// ── The rest: one macro per std::errc enumerator, same value ────────────
#define EPERM            1
#define ENOENT           2
#define ESRCH            3
#define EINTR            4
#define EIO              5
